 table←GetCommandTable path;list;names;ind;file;name;buff;desc;parse;project;Get
⍝ Scans the user command functions in `path` - the "UC" folder of the project - and returns a
⍝ matrix with one row per user command and four columns: name, `Parse`, `_Project`, `Desc`.\\
⍝ The metadata lives in the leading comments of the function itself:\\
⍝ ~~~
⍝ ⍝ Desc: Executes the git "Add" command
⍝ ⍝ Parse: 1 -project=
⍝ ⍝ Project: 0
⍝ ~~~
⍝ A function counts as a user command exactly when it carries a "Parse:" line, so the helpers
⍝ in "UC" are ignored without having to list them anywhere.\\
⍝ The result is sorted alphabetically, and every command must come with a help file.
 Get←{i←⍸(⊂⍺)≡¨(≢⍺)↑¨⍵ ⋄ 0=≢i:⎕NULL ⋄ {⍵↓⍨+/∧\' '=⍵}(≢⍺)↓(⊃i)⊃⍵}
 table←0 4⍴⊂''
 list←⊃##.APLGit2.FilesAndDirs.Dir path,'/*.aplf'
 names←{2⊃⎕NPARTS ⍵}¨list
 ind←⍋⎕C↑names
 list←list[ind]
 names←names[ind]
 :For file name :InEach list names
     buff←1↓⊃⎕NGET file 1
     :If 0<≢buff
         buff←{⍵↓⍨+/∧\' '=⍵}¨buff     ⍝ An indented leading comment is a leading comment, too
         buff←(∧\'⍝'=⊃¨buff)/buff      ⍝ The leading comments, and nothing else
     :EndIf
     parse←'⍝ Parse:'Get buff
     :If ⎕NULL≢parse                    ⍝ No "Parse:" means: not a user command but a helper
         desc←'⍝ Desc:'Get buff
         project←'⍝ Project:'Get buff
         ('No "Desc:" for the user command "',name,'"')Assert ⎕NULL≢desc
         ('No "Project:" for the user command "',name,'"')Assert ⎕NULL≢project
         ('No help file for the user command "',name,'"')Assert ⎕NEXISTS path,'/help/',name,'.apla'
         table⍪←name parse('1'≡⊃project)desc
     :EndIf
 :EndFor
 'No user commands found at all'Assert 0<≢table
⍝Done
